/* In-app guide */
"tool.guide.title" = "Guide";
"tool.guide.subtitle" = "How to use every tool, step by step";
"guide.intro.title" = "Welcome";
"guide.intro.body" = "NetToolbox is a modular network toolbox with 35 tools in four categories: Calculators, Diagnostics, Local Network and Professional. Everything runs natively on your device with no external servers except where a tool clearly fetches public data (public IP, speed test).\n\nOpen any tool from the home grid. Pull down to refresh where supported. Most results have a copy button; some have a share button.";
"guide.home.title" = "Home, search & themes";
"guide.home.body" = "• Search bar: type a tool name, or paste an IP / MAC / URL / domain — a smart suggestion card appears and takes you to the right tool.\n• Star (☆) on any card adds it to Favorites, which pin to the top.\n• Settings (top-right ⚙︎): pick one of six accent themes; your choice is saved.\n• The status pill shows your live connection: Wi-Fi / Cellular / Offline.";
"guide.tools.title" = "Key tools";
"guide.tools.body" = "• Subnet Calculator & VLSM: enter an IP with a CIDR (e.g. 192.168.1.10/24). VLSM splits a network into right-sized subnets.\n• Ping / Traceroute / IP Range Scanner: enter a host or CIDR. Ping is a TCP handshake timing (iOS blocks raw ICMP for ping); Traceroute and the Range Scanner use ICMP.\n• Port Scanner: choose Common/Web/All, or Custom and type e.g. 22,80,443,8000-8100.\n• DNS / nslookup / WHOIS / RBL / Email: DNS-based lookups. nslookup auto-detects forward vs reverse.\n• History: every Subnet/DNS/WHOIS/IP run is logged; copy, share or clear it.";
"guide.wifi.title" = "Wi-Fi info & the Shortcut (detailed)";
"guide.wifi.body" = "iOS does NOT let any app read Wi-Fi signal, channel, band, generation, link speed, or the SSID/BSSID. The Wi-Fi Info tool shows what IS allowed: connection type, device IP, and public IP.\n\nTo get the real SSID/BSSID, use the Shortcuts app (it has privileged access). Build it once:\n1. In Wi-Fi Info, tap \"Create it\" — the Shortcuts app opens a new shortcut.\n2. Add action \"Get Network Details\", set it to \"Wi-Fi Network Name\".\n3. Add \"Get Network Details\" again → \"Wi-Fi BSSID\".\n4. Add \"Get Network Details\" again → \"IP Address\".\n5. Add \"Get Contents of URL\" with URL: https://api.ipify.org\n6. Add \"Text\" and insert the values from steps 2–5 on separate lines.\n7. Add \"Show Result\".\n8. Name the shortcut EXACTLY: NetToolbox WiFi\n\nNow the \"Run Wi-Fi Shortcut\" button opens and runs it. First run asks for Location permission (needed to read the SSID) — allow it.";
"guide.localnet.title" = "Setup: Local Network capability";
"guide.localnet.body" = "For the LAN Scanner and IP Range Scanner to find anything, the host app must declare the Local Network capability. Do this once in Swift Playgrounds:\n\n1. In the Playgrounds sidebar, tap your app's name at the very top (e.g. \"NETPulse\") to open App Settings.\n2. Scroll to Capabilities and tap the + (Add) button.\n3. Choose \"Local Network\".\n4. Purpose String: type a reason, e.g. \"NetToolbox discovers devices and services on your local network.\" This text is shown in the iOS permission alert.\n5. Under \"Bonjour Service Types\", tap \"Add Service Type\" and add each of these (one per entry):\n_http._tcp\n_https._tcp\n_ssh._tcp\n_smb._tcp\n_airplay._tcp\n_raop._tcp\n_ipp._tcp\n_printer._tcp\n_googlecast._tcp\n_rfb._tcp\n_device-info._tcp\n_homekit._tcp\n6. Tap Add (top-right) to save the capability.\n\nAny service type you do NOT add here will never appear in scan results, so add all 12. The first time you open the LAN Scanner, iOS asks for Local Network access — tap Allow. Note: Traceroute to a public host (e.g. 8.8.8.8) does not need this capability.";
"guide.permissions.title" = "Permissions";
"guide.permissions.body" = "LAN Scanner (Bonjour) and IP Range Scanner need the iOS \"Local Network\" permission. When you first scan, iOS may prompt — allow it. If nothing is found, open iOS Settings → your app → enable Local Network. Without it these tools return empty (they don't crash).\n\nTools that reach the internet (Public IP, IP Info, Speed Test, DNS, WHOIS) need a working connection; they show a clear error when offline.";
"guide.pro.title" = "Professional: routers & devices";
"guide.pro.body" = "• SSH: a real, native SSH-2 client (curve25519-sha256 key exchange, aes256-gcm@openssh.com cipher, password auth), built entirely on CryptoKit — no external libraries. Enter host, port (22), username, password and a command; it opens a session, runs the one command, and shows the output. On first connect, check the host-key fingerprint it displays. No interactive shell yet, and only password auth.\n• Telnet: interactive plaintext terminal. Connect, then type commands. Unencrypted — trusted networks only.\n• FTP: connect + log in (default anonymous) and list a directory over passive mode. Plaintext.\n• MikroTik API: RouterOS API on port 8728. Enter host/user/password, then send commands like /system/resource/print.\n• SNMP: v2c GET for one OID, or Walk to enumerate a whole subtree. Configurable port and community; presets included.";
"guide.limits.title" = "Limits (Wi-Fi radio)";
"guide.limits.body" = "• SSH now works natively (see Professional) using CryptoKit's AES-GCM + curve25519 — it needs a server that offers aes256-gcm@openssh.com (nearly all modern OpenSSH do). It runs one command per session; there is no interactive shell or key-file auth yet, and SFTP is not built yet.\n• Wi-Fi radio details (signal/channel/band/generation/speed) and SSID/BSSID: blocked by iOS for all apps — use the Shortcut workaround above.\n• Traceroute may show \"*\" for routers that don't reply to ICMP.";
"guide.tests.title" = "Verify it works";
"guide.tests.body" = "Open Diagnostics → Engine Self-Tests and tap Run. It runs the same test vectors as the developer's unit tests — subnetting, IPv6, MAC/OUI, DNS, SNMP BER, Wake-on-LAN, Telnet, X.509 dates, base conversion, VLSM, passwords, input classification, IP ranges, FTP PASV, and more. All should pass on your device.";

